{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfcrbHnx4CbGLs9x5VxQ4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 07 is done, with one gap: 108 of the 115 values that rested on the customer search alone are now re-checked, and 7 at 125 × 125 were cut off by the time limit and still need long runs. No certified value changed and no false refutation turned up. No call at `value − 1` came back satisfiable, `benchmarks.corpus` still reads 6,374 / 6,376, and nothing was written to `solutions/`. The gate passed: Lean build, 1,356 MOSP tests and 110 pathwidth_solver tests.\n\n**The repaired solver.** I ran it (`repaired_rules=True`, in C) at `value − 1` on all 115, cheapest first, on 21 cores until 06:15.\n- **108 of 115 refuted**, which is 106 of the 113 distinct graphs. That is every listed instance at 40–100 customers and 16 of the 23 at 125 × 125: 9.7 × 10¹⁰ nodes, 16.5 core-hours.\n- Of the 21 hard ones items 04–05 had not done, 14 finished. The largest were `Random-100-100-2-3_0` at 2.1 × 10¹⁰ nodes (2.9 h) and `Random-125-125-4-3_0` at 3.7 × 10¹⁰ nodes (7.4 h).\n- **7 did not finish:** `Random-125-125-2-{1,4,5}` and `-4-{1,2,4,5}`, each stopped after 10.1 h at 5.0–6.9 × 10¹⁰ nodes. They keep their values as verified upper bounds and are not marked done. `paper2/solver_fix.md` lists them under \"Needs a long run\", priced at 13 to 153 core-hours each. Those prices are low, because both the cost model and the old recertify counts predate the 2026-09-26 fix that made these refutations larger. The `Random-100-100-2` runs cost 2.5–13× the cost model's figure. To continue them: `python -m paper2.solver_fix_recheck --stage repaired --retry-censored`. This restarts each search from scratch, because the search keeps no state between calls.\n\n**The cheaper check the item names mostly fails.** Checking `CodeNodeRepaired` along the old runs covers few of them:\n- Of 103 old refutations that finished, 33 pass at every node, so they are sound as originally run.\n- The other 70 fail at about 0.11% of their nodes: at the definite move, and also at the better move in all 8 runs with Theorem 2. A failing node only means the repaired premise wasn't met there, not that a solution was lost. All 70 are covered by the repaired re-refutations above.\n- Through the certificate emitter (capped at 2 × 10⁶ nodes), 58 old refutations fit. The independent checker verified all 58, and 32 pass the check.\n\n**Code change (search behaviour unchanged).** To check the old runs at C speed I added an audit mode: `cs_decide_rules(..., repaired_rules=2)`, exposed as `decide_native(audit_repaired=True)`. It runs the published rules unchanged and counts the nodes failing `CodeNodeRepaired`.\n- **Validation:** 36,126 calls on 6,005 small graphs with zero mismatches. It finds the same failing nodes as the certificate walk (which uses its own matching code), and that matching agreed with brute-force enumeration in 303,895 node checks.\n- On the 58 real certificates, the C and the walk give identical counts.\n- `pathwidth_solver/pathwidth/closing_search.c` is again a byte copy.\n- The new tests are in `tests/test_solver_fix_recheck.py`.\n\nI also wrote up, but did not build, a way to finish the seven: split one refutation across cores at the root. It needs a C entry point that starts from a given state, plus a short Lean statement that the pieces combine into a sound refutation.\n\nPROGRESS.md is updated (7/10) and item 07 is marked `[x]`. Nothing is committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1240,"cache_read_input_tokens":268280,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1240,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":825,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9a0accfe-dabc-426e-a5e6